  "slug": "arterial-domain-protein-2fbe",
  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect how flow, matrix proteins and ECM regulators direct vascular and tissue development and remodeling.",
  "synthesis": "The five preprints share a focus on mechanical and matrix-associated cues as determinants of vascular and tissue phenotype, although they differ in system and scope. In mouse neural crest lineages, Tmem2 deletion causes cleft palate with hyaluronan accumulation, hypoxia and vascular defects. Ilk deletion in embryonic renal stroma disrupts fibronectin expression and tissue integrity within a defined developmental window. In zebrafish, hemodynamic forces, klf2a/klf2b and Yap1/Wwtr1-dependent Vegfab-Vegfr2 signaling establish fenestrated endothelial identity in the choroid plexus. Spatial transcriptomics of ductus arteriosus from E14.5 to P2.5 tracks cell-cycle activity and the contractile-to-synthetic transition of mural cells during closure. In atherosclerotic mice, sustained LDL-C lowering expands CD26 and FMOD fibroblast subpopulations with distinct matrix programs, with KLF3 and FOXD1 as candidate regulators. What is new is the extension of this mechanical and matrix logic to ductus arteriosus remodeling and to fibroblast matrix programs during atherosclerosis remodeling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
